Environment
   Component   Version
  ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
   kimi CLI    1.40.0
   SDK         v0.0.0-20260421134432-b232049415ff
   Go          1.26.2
   Auth        OAuth (~/.kimi/credentials/kimi-code.json)
   OS          Linux
  Reproduction
  Run the exact example from the README:
  package main

  import (
      "context"
      "fmt"

      kimi "github.com/MoonshotAI/kimi-agent-sdk/go"
      "github.com/MoonshotAI/kimi-agent-sdk/go/wire"
  )

  func main() {
      session, err := kimi.NewSession(kimi.WithAutoApprove())
      if err != nil {
          panic(err)
      }
      defer session.Close()

      turn, err := session.Prompt(context.Background(), wire.NewStringContent("hello"))
      if err != nil {
          panic(err)
      }

      // This loop never completes — deadlock
      for step := range turn.Steps {
          for msg := range step.Messages {
              fmt.Printf("%T\n", msg)
          }
      }

      fmt.Println("Done") // never reached
  }
  Expected behavior
  turn.Steps should eventually close after all events are consumed, and the program should print Done.
  Actual behavior
  turn.Steps never closes. The program hangs indefinitely.
  • turn.Result().Status remains "pending" forever.
  • session.Close() also hangs.
  • Sending SIGQUIT (or pprof goroutine dump) reveals the following goroutine stack:
  goroutine [recv]:
    github.com/MoonshotAI/kimi-agent-sdk/go/wire/jsonrpc2.(*Codec).recv
        codec.go:389  // blocked on c.inreqs <- payload

  goroutine [ReadResponseHeader]:
    github.com/MoonshotAI/kimi-agent-sdk/go/wire/jsonrpc2.(*Codec).ReadResponseHeader
        codec.go:584  // blocked on c.inress
goroutine [Prompt RPC]:
    net/rpc.(*Client).Call
        client.go:321  // waiting for Prompt response
  Root cause analysis
  kimi CLI v1.40.0 emits two new event types during MCP initialization:
  {"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"event","params":{"type":"MCPLoadingBegin","payload":{}}}
  {"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"event","params":{"type":"MCPLoadingEnd","payload":{}}}
  However, the SDK's eventUnmarshaler map in wire/message.go does not contain handlers for MCPLoadingBegin or MCPLoadingEnd:
  var eventUnmarshaler = map[EventType]func(data []byte) (Event, error){
      // ...
      // MCPLoadingBegin and MCPLoadingEnd are MISSING
  }
  When ServeRequest tries to process one of these events, EventParams.UnmarshalJSON returns:
  unknown event type: "MCPLoadingBegin"
  This causes ReadRequestBody to fail, ServeRequest to exit with an error, and session.serve to stop consuming requests. The recv goroutine then blocks forever trying to push the next event into the now-unread c.inreqs channel. Bec
  ause recv is blocked, it can never decode the final PromptResult response, so rpc.Client.Call("Transport.Prompt") never returns — and turn.Steps is never closed.
  Verification
  I confirmed this by:
  1. Manual JSON-RPC test: Talking directly to kimi --wire with Python shows the full event stream is healthy, including MCPLoadingBegin → MCPLoadingEnd → TurnEnd → PromptResult(status=finished).
  2. SDK patch test: Adding the two missing event types to wire/message.go resolves the deadlock immediately.
  Suggested fix
  Add MCPLoadingBegin and MCPLoadingEnd to the SDK's event type registry:
  // wire/message.go
  type MCPLoadingBegin struct{}
  type MCPLoadingEnd struct{}

  func (MCPLoadingBegin) message() {}
  func (MCPLoadingEnd) message()   {}

  func (MCPLoadingBegin) EventType() EventType { return EventTypeMCPLoadingBegin }
  func (MCPLoadingEnd) EventType() EventType   { return EventTypeMCPLoadingEnd }

  const (
      // ...
      EventTypeMCPLoadingBegin EventType = "MCPLoadingBegin"
      EventTypeMCPLoadingEnd   EventType = "MCPLoadingEnd"
  )

  var eventUnmarshaler = map[EventType]func(data []byte) (Event, error){
      // ...
      EventTypeMCPLoadingBegin: unmarshalEvent[MCPLoadingBegin],
      EventTypeMCPLoadingEnd:   unmarshalEvent[MCPLoadingEnd],
  }
  Alternatively, making EventParams.UnmarshalJSON gracefully skip unknown event types (with a warning or log) instead of returning a hard error would prevent future CLI versions from breaking the SDK.
